{"type": "mcq", "stem": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For triangles <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n\t<mi>G</mi>\n\t<mi>H</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, angles <math alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi>\n</math> and <math alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi>\n</math> each measure <math alttext=\"51 degree\"><mn>51</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn>\n</math>, and the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper F upper G\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn>\n</math>. Which additional piece(s) of information is(are) sufficient to prove that triangle <math alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>A</mi>\n\t<mi>B</mi>\n\t<mi>C</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math> is similar to triangle <math alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>F</mi>\n\t<mi>G</mi>\n\t<mi>H</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>?</p>\n<ol style=\"list-style-type: upper-roman;\">\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi>\n</math> is equal to the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi>\n</math>.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">The measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi>\n</math> is equal to the measure of angle <math alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi>\n</math>.</li>\n<li style=\"text-align: left;\">The length of&nbsp;<math alttext=\"line segment upper F upper H\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math> times the length of <math alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>.</li>\n</ol>", "keys": ["5dabd084-9d06-4812-b93f-e135f5ca2aad"], "answerOptions": [{"id": "9e858197-7601-42bf-80d6-bcc8c8c4f2b6", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">I</span> is sufficient, <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">II</span> is sufficient, and <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">III</span> is sufficient.</p>"}, {"id": "5dabd084-9d06-4812-b93f-e135f5ca2aad", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">I</span> is sufficient and <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">II</span> is sufficient, but <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">III</span> is not.</p>"}, {"id": "eb8ba837-a09b-495e-acdb-f092820f436c", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">II</span> is sufficient and <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">III</span> is sufficient, but <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">I</span> is not.</p>"}, {"id": "bfbb6b09-ac04-4def-a7fc-29e85f1e43a8", "content": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">III</span> is sufficient, but <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">I</span> is not and <span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">II</span> is not.</p>"}], "rationale": "<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that in triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math>, angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math> each measure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"51 degree\"><mn>51</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>, the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math>, and the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper F upper G\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math>. For statement I, if the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> is equal to the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math>, then triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> have two pairs of congruent angles, angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A\"><mi>A</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F\"><mi>F</mi></math> and angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math>. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn></math> pairs of angles in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn></math> triangles are congruent, the triangles are similar, so statement I is sufficient. For statement II, if the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> is equal to the measure of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi></math>, then triangles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi><mi>H</mi></math> have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn></math> pairs of congruent angles, angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper C\"><mi>C</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper H\"><mi>H</mi></math> and angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math>. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn></math> pairs of angles in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn></math> triangles are congruent, the triangles are similar, so statement II is sufficient. For statement III, if the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper F upper H\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math> times the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>, then the ratio of the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper F upper H\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;to the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper C\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper F upper G\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"18\"><mn>18</mn></math> and the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"6\"><mn>6</mn></math>. Therefore, the ratio of the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper F upper G\"><mover><mrow><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math> to the length of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"line segment upper A upper B\"><mover><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>&#175;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn></math>, so there are two pairs of sides in proportion. However, angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper B\"><mi>B</mi></math> isn&rsquo;t between sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper B\"><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper A upper C\"><mi>A</mi><mi>C</mi></math>, and angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper G\"><mi>G</mi></math> isn&rsquo;t between sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper G\"><mi>F</mi><mi>G</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"upper F upper H\"><mi>F</mi><mi>H</mi></math>. Since the given angle measures aren&rsquo;t between the proportional sides, this information isn't sufficient to prove that the triangles are similar. Therefore, statements I and II are each sufficient, but statement III isn't sufficient.</p>\n<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>", "externalid": "ba093354-b8dd-457d-94ec-dc6f8b6136ff", "correct_answer": ["B"]}